ormats including  novels shakespeare plays and even fairy  tales  i just wish i knew about it when i was  back in school chumley calls cgc  grading's paul lynch for a consultation  lich says this particular comic is known  for a rare variant interior cover which  includes what he calls quotes a free  promo kind of thing  unfortunately zane's issue turns out to  be the standard version but it doesn't  go away empty-handed  in spite of the book's dog-eared  condition lynch's estimate isn't far  below the asking price  lynch puts the value of the book at 550  and after some hagglings and lets it go  for 375  in season 11 a hopeful seller brings a  couple of valuable comics to the gold  and silver pawn shop  one of them is 1975's giant size x-men  number one for which he's asking 1200.  he tells rick he traded some original  art for the valuable comic though he  doesn't go into more  detail while marvel's x-men first  appeared in 1963's uncanny x-men number  one many of the franchise's most popular  mutants are introduced in giant size  x-men number one nightcrawler storm  colossus and magic all make their first  appearances in the later comic while  wolverine was first glimpsed a year  earlier in the final panel of incredible  hulk number 180 it wasn't until giant  size x-men number one that we learned  he's a mutant or saw the surly attitude  he's famous for  this kid's crying do something rick  calls paul lytch to grade the comics but  the seller clearly isn't happy with  lich's estimate on giant size x-men's  value  while they agree on the grade the  appraiser puts a value between 550 and  600  less than half the asking price when the  seller says he needs at least  900 dollars for it rick doesn't even  bother trying to haggle and passes on it  entirely  oh i love it when they play hard to get  in the same episode as giant size x-men  number one the seller also brings in a  copy of 1963's avengers number one the  very first story about the marvel comics  team that grew to be a media powerhouse  he asked rick for a whopping 4 600 for  the historic issue  that's the best you can do when rick  calls paul lynch and to give his two  cents the expert explains that the  avengers owe a large part of their  existence to a missed deadline  when writer bill everett fell behind on  his deadline for daredevil number one  stan lee and jack kirby quickly  brainstormed a team featuring some of  their most popular superheroes  thor the hulk ant-man the wasp and iron  man ironically although you could argue  daredevil is the reason the avengers  exist  it took until 2011's new avengers number  16 for old hornhead to join the team  well i guess we're both disappointed  then lych places the value of avengers  number one  barely close to the seller's asking  price between four thousand and forty  two hundred dollars  unfortunately rick and the seller can't  come to an agreement rick offers to buy  it for thirty two hundred dollars the  seller goes down as far as thirty three  hundred dollars and neither will budge  to give you an idea of how many factors  contribute to a rare comic book's price  tag  three years before this scene a high  grade copy of avengers number one sold  for 274  850 none of this makes sense  season 13 silver stash pawn shows us not  only one of the most valuable comics to  appear on pawn stars but one of the most  unexpected  in this episode dan returns with a rare  copy of 1950's adventures of bob hope  number one  after world war ii the popularity of  superhero comics waned  and didn't rise again until the 60s in  that time companies like dc looked to  new directions for comic books including  licensed celebrity titles like  adventures of bob hope most issues of  the series featured hope in a series of  comically crazy calamities similar to  his movies  paul lynch comes by and in a rare turn  of events values the comic at 600  100 higher than zan's asking price rick  seems doubtful the comic will sell  considering that bob hope isn't an  action hero and isn't even a comedian  most younger people would recognize  he haggled zan down to 325 do you know  who bob hope is the price is right  [Music]  while bob hope may seem like a bizarre  subject for a comic book  keep in mind that 109 issues of  adventures of bob hope were published  between 1950 and 1968.  considering most comics don't even hit  their 100th issue these days  adventures of bob hope must have been  doing something right  i'm rich whether it's genuine skepticism  or just his famous poker face  rick rarely seems impressed with comics  but that changes when a woman named  holly presents him with a copy of 1952's  mad number one  holly asked for 2 500 for the comic so  rick calls in paul lytch once again  that's awesome originally published by  ec comics mad released 23 issues before  switching to its more well-known  magazine format  there's some controversy over whether  this change was made to avoid the  notorious censorship of the comics code  authority  or just to prevent writer and artist  harvey kurtzman from jumping ship  regardless of why it stopped being a  comic book lynch agrees that mad number  one is a rare find and values it at two  thousand dollars  holly ultimately refuses to part with it  asking for fifteen hundred dollars while  rick won't go about fourteen hundred  dollars  sometimes a unique bind requires the  gold and silver pawn shop to consult  more than one expert  in season 14 a young woman named lindsay  brings in a copy of 1977 star wars  number three hoping to use the money to  pay for her 4 000  wedding dress what makes this comic  different is that the cover includes  signatures from harrison ford carrie  fisher and mark hamill  to make sure the signatures aren't  forged chumley brings in authenticator  steve grad  he says that not only are the autographs  genuine but he knows exactly when and  where they come from  a 1977 signing at a canadian comic book  shop  he gives an estimate of three t